What was reused, and how
------------------------

Reused with modification (attribution required, MIT satisfied by LICENSE):

  internal/web/assets/styles.css
      Derived from Tempik src/web/styles.css. The design tokens, layout,
      component styling, and responsive rules are unchanged.
      Local changes: the external Google Fonts `@import` was removed so the
      embedded asset bundle is self-contained and does not send visitor IPs to a
      third-party CDN (the existing font stack already falls back to system
      fonts); Blinkmail-specific rules for form labels, the public-address
      notice, the error banner, focus-visible rings, and busy states were
      appended at the end of the file.

  internal/web/assets/index.html
      Page structure, section order, element IDs and class names follow Tempik
      src/web/index.html.
      Local changes: rebranded to Blinkmail; labels and ARIA attributes added to
      every control; a public-address notice and a dedicated error banner added;
      the favicon letter changed from "T" to "B"; the footer links to Blinkmail
      and credits Tempik and its author.

  internal/web/assets/app.js
      The workflow is ported from Tempik src/web/app.js: anonymous browser
      sessions, custom and random address creation, domain selection, inbox
      switching, copying the address, refreshing messages, and removing an inbox
      from the session.
      Local change that is deliberately NOT a port: the reference rendered
      messages by interpolating `from_address`, `subject`, and `body` directly
      into `innerHTML`, so HTML or script in an incoming email executed in the
      reader's browser. Blinkmail builds every message node with
      `document.createElement` and `textContent` instead, and error text goes
      through the same path. Also added: a Blinkmail-specific localStorage key,
      session-rotation recovery, busy states, clipboard fallback for
      non-secure contexts, and honest wording on inbox removal.

  internal/address/address.go
      The FIRST/SECOND word lists and the "two words plus an optional
      two-digit suffix" local-part shape are ported from Tempik
      src/utils/random-address.ts.
      Local changes: randomness comes from crypto/rand rather than Math.random
      (a guessable address would let a stranger read a public inbox); the
      timestamp-based collision fallback was replaced with random digits.

  internal/store/migrations/001_init.sql
      The four-table model (inboxes, messages, sessions, session_inboxes),
      the column names, and the index set follow Tempik src/db/schema.sql.
      Local changes: added `messages.delivery_hash UNIQUE` for deduplicating
      repeated Email Worker deliveries; made `subject` and `body` NOT NULL so a
      parser cannot bypass their defaults with NULL; timestamps use RFC 3339
      UTC instead of `datetime('now')`, whose zone-less output browsers read as
      local time.

  internal/api/handlers.go, internal/api/server.go
      Routes, HTTP methods, JSON key names (`appName`, `mailDomain`,
      `mailDomains`, `webHost`, `sessionId`, `address`, `created_at`, `id`,
      `inbox_address`, `from_address`, `subject`, `body`, `received_at`, `ok`),
      status codes, and the `{ "error": "..." }` envelope follow Tempik
      src/api/routes.ts.
      Local changes: session tokens are server-generated and validated instead of
      being arbitrary client-supplied strings; local parts and domains are
      validated; the reference's double `decodeURIComponent` on the address path
      is dropped (net/http already decodes path values).

Reimplemented rather than ported:

  internal/mail/mail.go — MIME parsing via github.com/emersion/go-message with
      its charset package, replacing Tempik's postal-mime usage. Handles
      RFC 2047 encoded subjects, multipart nesting, transfer encodings, and
      non-UTF-8 character sets. Attachments are ignored by design; plain text is
      preferred, and HTML-only content is stored as text for the frontend to
      render inertly.

  relay/worker.js — a Cloudflare Email Worker that forwards raw MIME to the Go
      application over HTTPS. It replaces Tempik's in-Worker parse-and-store
      handler. Failures are reported honestly: permanent conditions call
      `message.setReject()`, transient ones throw so Cloudflare may retry. The
      reference caught every error, logged it, and returned normally, which made
      Cloudflare acknowledge a message it had actually lost.

Not reused:

  The Tempik name, its "T" favicon, and its "Developer by masantoid" footer
  branding. MIT does not convey trademark rights, so Blinkmail is branded
  separately while still crediting the original author and project.

